  "answer": "The public claim that PDFs support precise page- and paragraph-level citations needs a major caveat: this capability is highly dependent on document quality, especially for scanned, photocopied, or image-only PDFs [NL-002#NL-002-S09].\n\n- Scanned, photocopied, or image-only PDFs do not contain native text layers and must go through OCR; if the scan is blurry, low-resolution, or has complex layouts, OCR can produce broken or incomplete text blocks, leading to missing citations, incorrect page links, or TutorChat saying it cannot find the answer even when the information exists [NL-002#NL-002-S09].\n- Without OCR, a scanned PDF's parser extracts zero text and citations cannot be generated at all [NL-006#NL-006-S03].\n- In practice, for scanned image-only PDFs, TutorChat often links back to the start of the file rather than the specific page, or fails to generate a citation link entirely [NL-011#NL-011-S05].\n\nSo the help article should clarify that precise page/paragraph citations are only reliable for natively digital, text-searchable PDFs, and that scanned image-only PDFs depend on OCR quality and may produce degraded or missing citations.\n\nANSWER_STATUS: answer\nUSED_MEMORY_CLAIMS: none",
